WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:01.383
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:01.383 --> 00:00:02.990
Sal Khan here from Khan Academy.

00:00:02.990 --> 00:00:04.990
Welcome to the Homeroom livestream.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.710
We're very excited to have a conversation

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:12.320
with U.S. Secretary of
Education Miguel Cardona today.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.250
But before we jump into that conversation,

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.060
I will remind you a few
of my typical reminders.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.590
First of all, Khan Academy is
a not-for-profit organization.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.190
We can only exist through donations

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:23.300
from folks like yourself.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.000
So if you're in a position to do so,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.420
please go to khanacademy.org/donate.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.140
I also wanna make a special shout out

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.030
to several organizations
that have stepped up

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.740
especially during the pandemic.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:36.960
Many of y'all know that our
traffic tripled a year ago

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:39.200
when we went into the pandemic.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.760
A lot of more folks
have been leaning on us.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.050
Our server costs went up.

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:44.590
And so, special thanks
to Bank of America, AT&amp;T,

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:47.740
Google.org, Novartis,
Fastly, and General Motors,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.980
and many other supporters of Khan Academy

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:52.230
for stepping up and making
sure that we're in a position

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.470
to support millions, tens
of millions of learners

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:55.740
around the world.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.540
So thank you for that.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.520
And I also wanna remind
folks that there is a version

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.580
of this livestream in podcast form

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.400
wherever you get your podcasts,

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.940
Homeroom with Sal, the Podcast.

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:08.700
So please check that out.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:11.020
So with that, I am very excited

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.630
to introduce Dr. Miguel Cardona,

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.370
U.S. Secretary of Education.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.600
Secretary Cardona, thank
you for joining us.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.650
- Hey, happy to be here in Homeroom.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:21.500
Happy to be here.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.570
- So maybe a great place
for folks to start,

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.670
I think people know
Secretary of Education,

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:29.800
federal government,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:34.140
it's the most senior education
leader in the country.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.590
But from your vantage point,
what do you see as the role,

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:41.810
the job, the potential of
U.S. Secretary of Education?

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.520
- You know, supporting kids,

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.520
supporting students from pre-K
to higher education really.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.370
It's really making sure,

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.020
I'd like to think this role is really,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.831
it really needs to be

00:01:54.831 --> 00:01:57.100
the greatest child
advocate in the country,

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.830
to make sure that our education program

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.040
is meeting the needs
of all of our students,

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.440
the diverse needs of our students,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:05.290
and making sure that
we're letting education

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.493
be that great equalizer
that it was for me.

00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:09.960
- And how do you view that?

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.880
Do you view it as, is it advocacy?

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.760
What are the levers at your disposal?

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.937
The U.S. famously has a very, you know,

00:02:15.937 --> 00:02:18.370
it's a very federated system in education.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:19.940
A lot of the resources
are happening at the state

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.310
and the local government.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.870
Where does your role fit
in in the whole ecosystem?

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:26.703
- Sure.

00:02:26.703 --> 00:02:28.510
So, you know, working with states,

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.840
working with local districts
to help support them.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.210
Right now as we recover from this pandemic

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:38.070
there is more funding coming
from the federal government.

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.580
But, you know, making sure
that we're using this position

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.090
to support, to elevate best practices,

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:44.880
to use the position as a bully pulpit

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.100
around those things that
we know work for kids

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.440
and those things that we
should be doing in education,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.770
but also at the policy level too

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.530
making sure that we're advocating

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:55.753
and also protecting
the rights of students.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.120
They deserve a free and
appropriate public education

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.900
throughout our country.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:03.040
- And I definitely wanna get into

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.170
some of those policy questions.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.490
And I think it's an exciting time

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.020
to be in education generally.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:08.500
I'm imagining a very exciting time

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:09.660
to be Secretary of Education.

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:10.520
There's so much to do.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.230
There's never been more energy,
I think, behind education,

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.710
potentially more funding behind education

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.340
is what we're starting to see.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.390
But before we jump into
that, I wanna remind everyone

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.340
you can ask your questions
on YouTube and Facebook.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.310
I'll try to surface as many
of the questions as we can

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.820
to Secretary Cardona.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:27.390
But I'd like to know a little
bit about your life journey.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.370
I don't think a lot of
kids wake up in the morning

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:30.930
when they're seven years old and say,

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.850
"I will be U.S. Secretary
of Education in 2021."

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:34.960
So what was that pathway?

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.920
What got you here?

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:38.210
- Listen, I grew up.

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:39.520
My parents came from Puerto Rico

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.050
when they were in their
teens and, you know,

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.900
my grandparents moved for a better life.

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.110
My grandfather cut sugar
cane in Puerto Rico.

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:51.550
And, you know, the money was
paying a little bit better

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:54.490
in Hartford, Connecticut,
where you cut tobacco.

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:56.040
So he settled in Meriden.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.810
He left the beautiful
paradise island over there,

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.460
palm trees and beaches, to
go live on the fourth floor

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.120
of apartment projects in a place where

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:06.750
he didn't know the language,
didn't know the culture.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:09.480
But he did that so that he
could provide for his family.

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:11.630
My parents were born.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.030
They lived in that same community.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.673
And early on, Sal,

00:04:16.252 --> 00:04:18.630
I was modeled service to community.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:21.140
And the schools gave me so much,

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.040
so I ended up becoming an educator.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.290
But service to the community

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:25.830
was really what I wanted
to do for my life.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:28.140
And it took me from a classroom teacher,

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.170
fourth grade teacher, to
Secretary of Education.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:32.730
But at the end of the
day, it's still service.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.460
- And fill in the blanks a little bit.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.190
'Cause you said, you know,
service, classroom teacher,

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:39.500
Secretary of Education.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.020
But how does that happen?

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:43.290
You know, did you go into administration?

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:44.810
What was that journey like?

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:47.210
- I went to a technical high school

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:50.310
because, you know, we had
a big family in Meriden.

00:04:50.310 --> 00:04:53.170
And my eldest cousin went
to a technical high school,

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:54.890
so all the little cousins
followed, you know,

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:56.610
like little ducks crossing the road.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:57.820
And it was a great experience.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:00.160
I learned automotive
technology for four years.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.047
And I had a teacher that
said, "You know, Miguel,

00:05:02.047 --> 00:05:04.150
"you should consider teaching."

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.860
So I wanted to go become an art teacher.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.820
And I went to college thinking

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.360
I was gonna become an art teacher.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.740
I loved the arts.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:14.000
And again, teaching is an
extension of service, right?

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.530
So I went into college,

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:18.010
but I gravitated toward
elementary education.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:19.450
I became a fourth grade teacher.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:20.800
I loved that role.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.740
I never wanted to leave the classroom.

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.190
But I got asked and
pushed into administration

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:27.100
by the superintendent at the time.

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:30.070
Then I became an elementary
school principal for 10 years,

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.910
loved that role, really
engaged with families.

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.520
I served students with disabilities.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.430
I served students that
were learning English

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.940
as a second language.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.000
We had children with developmental delays.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.300
We had children with multiple handicaps.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.530
It was a really wonderful
learning experience for me.

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:49.170
I became an assistant
superintendent in that district.

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:51.640
I did a little work teaching
at the college level.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.700
And then, the governor
of Connecticut called

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.760
for me to serve as
Commissioner of Education.

00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:57.990
And I enjoyed that role.

00:05:57.990 --> 00:05:59.810
I'm really passionate about addressing

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:01.820
inequities in education.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.860
And then the pandemic hit.

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.320
And, you know, we worked really hard

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:08.760
to try to safely reopen
schools in Connecticut.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:11.440
And that work and that passion

00:06:12.500 --> 00:06:14.850
got me a call from the
president of the United States.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.730
So now, I'm fortunate to be serving

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:19.623
as Secretary of Education.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.310
- You know, I definitely wanna get into

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:24.980
what you hope to do in this role now

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:26.510
and some of the policy questions.

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:28.630
But I wanna double click again on this.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:31.000
Just talking to you, I can
sense some of your energy

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.510
and the passion for what you
do, so it's not surprising.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:35.310
But what do you think it was?

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.670
You know, what do you think
it was about that teacher

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:40.070
that said that maybe you
should become a teacher?

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:41.310
What did they see in you?

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.320
And then, what do you think
other folks have seen in you

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.770
or you saw in yourself
that allowed you to go

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:49.150
on this very, very impressive journey?

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.170
- You know, what's special about me

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.110
is that there's nothing special about me,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.400
that I represent every
student in the classroom

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.330
that, you know, just wants to
do well and wants to serve.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.600
And I had a teacher just realize

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.510
that I was using my artwork
to communicate my values.

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:05.360
So there was a mural that I did, Sal.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:11.130
Back then, you know, it was
like late 80s, early 90s.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:12.747
And I did a mural that said,

00:07:12.747 --> 00:07:14.720
"There's only one race, the human race."

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.000
And I had in that mural all
these different characters

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.200
from different backgrounds

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.670
and different cultural
traditions, different dress.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:23.647
And the teacher said, "You know,

00:07:23.647 --> 00:07:27.450
"why don't you make that
a mural in the cafeteria?"

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.830
So she saw that I was using my art

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.453
to communicate my values.

00:07:33.507 --> 00:07:34.467
So she gave me that opportunity

00:07:34.467 --> 00:07:37.450
and that really made me feel
like: Well, I can do this.

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:39.740
And it's important to
name those folks by name.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:43.830
So Linda Ransom was that
teacher who saw that in me

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:48.150
and said, "Why don't you
consider a career in education?"

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:49.150
And that's what did it.

00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.510
So it was really a teacher
seeing something in me

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:55.070
that I can communicate through arts.

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:56.600
And that's all it took.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:58.000
And I was hooked after that.

00:07:59.889 --> 00:08:02.180
- I'm so glad you
highlighted it in that way.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:03.200
Because it's very easy to say:

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:04.510
What does the governor see in you?

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:07.780
Or what did a school leader see in you?

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:09.110
But you're so right.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:11.710
It's those teachers early on in your life

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:13.580
that when you might, you know, for me

00:08:13.580 --> 00:08:14.830
and you're right, it's
important to name them.

00:08:14.830 --> 00:08:16.870
Octavio Hernandez, my algebra teacher,

00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:19.390
and he was our mentor for the Math Club.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.260
I was a little bit of a math nerd.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:22.733
- (laughs) Just a little, right.

00:08:22.733 --> 00:08:24.960
- And Dr. Santanilla who
was a professor at UNO.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:26.510
And I didn't have a computer at home,

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:28.600
and he let me use his
computers while I was doing

00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:30.200
some dual enrollment
things in high school.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.720
So you're absolutely right.

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:33.370
It's those teachers that make

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:36.000
all the difference in the world.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:37.440
And all sorts of questions are coming in.

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:38.490
So I wanna make sure I have time

00:08:38.490 --> 00:08:39.380
to get to all these questions.

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:43.100
But, you know, now that
you are in this role

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:48.100
and it's still the first few
months of your tenure here,

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:49.400
what are your hopes and fears?

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:50.280
What are you hoping?

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.290
And, you know, if we look
back on this in 10 years,

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.770
it's like: Yes, we were
able to do X, Y, and Z.

00:08:54.770 --> 00:08:58.380
And what are your fears on top of that?

00:08:58.380 --> 00:09:00.413
- You know, 59 days to be exact.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.860
But we hit the ground running

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:05.110
and we're gonna continue running.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:07.040
I know that there's a finite window

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.110
to serve as Secretary of
Education and there's such need.

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:11.450
Since we've been collecting data,

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.540
there have been gaps in
outcomes for students.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.800
And we have to make sure that
we're gonna address that.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:18.190
I don't think there's ever been a time

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:20.510
in the history of our education system

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:24.310
where we've been as close to
a reset button as we are now.

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:27.840
So yes, the pandemic really
did a number on our country

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.290
and it's gonna take time
to reel in from that.

00:09:32.290 --> 00:09:35.580
But I wanna seize this
opportunity to rethink

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.310
some of those things that we've been doing

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.620
that are maybe not best for kids,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:45.430
or maybe really just re-imagine
how we can reopen our doors

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.630
not only physically and
make sure the lights are on

00:09:47.630 --> 00:09:50.630
but just rethink how we're
educating our students

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:52.290
and how we're partnering with families

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.940
and how we're listening to students

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:56.140
in this process of
reopening and recovering.

00:09:57.220 --> 00:09:58.200
- Are there certain areas

00:09:58.200 --> 00:10:00.020
that you are particularly excited about?

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:03.140
You're right, you know, with
the pandemic, and education,

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.820
I've been in this game
for a little while as well

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:08.250
and I've never seen so much
energy behind education

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:10.710
because it's a bit of a reboot, reset

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:11.990
because of the pandemic.

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:13.960
But where do you see as the opportunities

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:16.150
and where do you see
as the risks right now?

00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:16.983
- Sure.

00:10:16.983 --> 00:10:18.550
So tremendous opportunities.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:21.280
You know, I'm talking to an
innovative genius here, right?

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:24.000
I remember hearing your
name in a cafeteria

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:27.110
at Hanover School over 10, 12 years ago

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.650
where a teacher said, "Hey,
check out these videos."

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:31.340
And look at now.

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:32.850
You know, my kids,

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:35.440
Khan Academy is a topic
at the dinner table

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:39.540
at most houses where
I've been an educator.

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:42.340
So how do we allow
innovation to really drive

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:43.510
where we're going?

00:10:43.510 --> 00:10:47.080
How do we kind of, you
know, revisit those things

00:10:47.080 --> 00:10:51.650
that are sacred cows that maybe are not

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:53.650
in the best interest of students?

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:56.150
So when we're looking
at time, time in seats,

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:57.840
how we utilize technology,

00:10:57.840 --> 00:10:59.420
how we use blended learning.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:00.690
You know, blended learning is something

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.490
that happened overnight, and
we're just getting better.

00:11:03.490 --> 00:11:05.040
How we're closing the digital divide

00:11:05.040 --> 00:11:07.910
to make sure we have
greater access to learning

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.820
so that I'm not limited to the experiences

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:12.410
that I have in my community
or in my schoolhouse.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:14.720
You know, those are some
areas of innovation.

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:16.220
We can have students
connecting with students

00:11:16.220 --> 00:11:18.900
across the country, just
like I'm connecting with you.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:21.190
Some of the fears, some of the fears.

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:25.270
So while we're pleased
to see that about 80%

00:11:25.270 --> 00:11:27.710
of the pre-K-eight schools have some form

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:29.660
of in-person learning at this point,

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:33.460
we know that the data is
disproportionate by race.

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:38.060
We know that Black and Brown
students and Asian students

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.960
are less likely to go into school

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:41.590
even if that option is there.

00:11:41.590 --> 00:11:44.580
So what we've seen
happen in this past year

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:47.753
is a gross exacerbation of opportunities

00:11:47.753 --> 00:11:52.340
and outcomes for our students
based on not only race

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:53.850
but also where they live, right?

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:57.300
So rural communities have
been impacted significantly

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:00.140
based on the fact that
the internet connection

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:01.390
maybe isn't as strong.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:03.160
So we have a lot of work to do there.

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.390
And my fear is that we don't
maintain the level of urgency

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:10.940
that we need to really
reshape where we're going.

00:12:10.940 --> 00:12:12.990
That would be my greatest fear

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:16.163
that our urgency dissipates over time.

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:21.250
- Yeah, no, completely
understand that last notion.

00:12:21.250 --> 00:12:22.800
You know, there's a group of,

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:24.470
just through the work
we do at Khan Academy,

00:12:24.470 --> 00:12:26.720
of superintendents and
state commissioners,

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:28.052
your old job,

00:12:28.052 --> 00:12:29.470
that we've connected with.

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:32.330
And I even get 'em together on
a Zoom every other Friday now

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:33.920
to discuss some of these topics.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.900
And they're all feeling
exactly what you're feeling.

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:38.620
This is an unusual moment.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:40.520
The pandemic has been incredibly hard,

00:12:40.520 --> 00:12:42.010
especially on some of the communities

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:45.490
that, frankly, already
needed some extra support

00:12:45.490 --> 00:12:48.070
because of various contexts pre-pandemic.

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:51.020
And now, there's a unique opportunity

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:54.060
to hopefully solve the problems

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.770
that the pandemic introduced,
but also move ahead.

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:59.810
And there's incredible
federal dollars coming way.

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:03.070
There's the third round of
stimulus had a lot of dollars

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:04.810
that are coming from
the federal government.

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:07.640
And I know y'all are proposing
more that I wanna talk about.

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:09.880
Where do you see, have you seen examples?

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:11.660
Are there things in the
back of y'all's mind

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:12.730
that you see it like:

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:14.610
That's going to be a really
good use of these funds.

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:18.220
I think it averages $800 or
$900 per student in America.

00:13:18.220 --> 00:13:20.090
And in certain areas,

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:21.820
it might be as much as $2,000 or $3,000.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:25.140
So it's significant resources
for the next three years.

00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:26.440
- You know, I have to start

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:29.450
with social emotional
well-being of students.

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:31.800
What I'm finding is that

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:33.680
we are still in the middle of a pandemic.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:35.987
And we can't think that just
because the lights are on

00:13:35.987 --> 00:13:37.160
and the hallways are clean

00:13:37.160 --> 00:13:38.720
and the doorknobs have been wiped down

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.460
that we can go back to business as usual.

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:43.990
Many of our students have
experienced a lot of loss

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:47.950
whether it's loss of loved
ones or, you know, loss of jobs

00:13:47.950 --> 00:13:50.270
or their parents being underemployed now

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:53.200
or having to change and
not seeing loved ones

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:54.560
for a long period of time.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:56.740
So we have to make sure
that when we reopen schools,

00:13:56.740 --> 00:14:00.220
we're ready and prepared to
meet our students where they are

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.510
and provide them that
support that they need

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:03.790
to be emotionally safe

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.600
so that bandwidth for learning can grow.

00:14:06.600 --> 00:14:09.350
So that's one of the areas
where I see that that's working.

00:14:09.350 --> 00:14:13.040
Another area that I'm noticing
with the Help is Here tour

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:14.180
is that in those places

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:18.070
where there's intentional
collaboration, right,

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:20.769
so collaboration that's deeper

00:14:20.769 --> 00:14:22.960
than it was prior to the pandemic,

00:14:22.960 --> 00:14:25.540
those are the places that
are reopening quicker

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:29.990
and more safe, and allowing for innovation

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:31.710
to take root earlier.

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:34.580
So it's really some of those
underlying things like that,

00:14:34.580 --> 00:14:36.530
social emotional
well-being of our students,

00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:40.610
of our educators, but also
making sure that we reorganize

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:43.750
so that we're more responsive
to the needs of the community.

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:45.080
That's how we're gonna
get students back in.

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:47.330
And that's how we're gonna
get families to re-engage.

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:51.180
So funds for educators to
help lower class sizes,

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:53.290
especially for those students
that were hit hardest

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:54.940
by the pandemic and maybe haven't been

00:14:54.940 --> 00:14:56.630
in a school house for over a year,

00:14:56.630 --> 00:15:00.360
more social workers,
more school counselors,

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:03.420
better tools for teachers,
better training for teachers.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:05.170
You know, we keep asking teachers

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:06.930
to do more and more and more.

00:15:06.930 --> 00:15:08.160
We need to make sure we embed

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:11.110
professional learning opportunities,
in a creative way too.

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:14.790
It can't be just packing
more into those three PD days

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:15.780
that they get.

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:17.440
So we really need to rethink those things.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:19.580
But those states that are using the funds

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:21.870
to provide social emotional support,

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:25.070
good afterschool programming
that are hands-on,

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:26.640
good summer learning opportunities

00:15:26.640 --> 00:15:28.530
for students to get out into the community

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:32.160
and do camp-like activities
and learn with their hands

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:34.310
and discovery learning,
those are the places

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:37.190
that are finding the most
success with reopening.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.720
But those are the ones that are
really utilizing their funds

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:41.570
to address the needs of the students.

00:15:42.478 --> 00:15:45.530
- You know, I love that answer
because I think in education,

00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:46.930
because the things we measure are often

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:49.770
things like test scores
in reading or math,

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:51.487
we can become very
obsessed with test scores.

00:15:51.487 --> 00:15:53.290
And it is, at least in
my opinion, important

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:55.770
to measure these things to
understand where you are.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.540
But when we face a situation like this,

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:59.040
it's very tempting to immediately get into

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:00.890
how do we get back, fill in the gaps,

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:01.910
fill in the academic gaps.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:04.140
But to your point, and
I've seen this as a father

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.080
and we've seen this in
the work at Khan Academy,

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:08.210
if you don't have, if the kids aren't

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:10.630
in the right place from a
social emotional point of view,

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:13.050
if there isn't the right
collaboration amongst the adults,

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:15.130
there's not the right
training amongst the adults,

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:18.150
it doesn't matter what tools you provide.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:20.370
It doesn't matter what
curricula you provide.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:21.770
You're not going to get the same outcomes.

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:22.640
And the other way around,

00:16:22.640 --> 00:16:24.560
if you do have good social emotional,

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:26.740
if you do have good collaboration,

00:16:26.740 --> 00:16:29.010
it also does not matter what
kind of curricula you have.

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:30.740
You'll probably have a good outcome.

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:33.560
So I think that's a very powerful notion.

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:34.393
- Absolutely.

00:16:34.393 --> 00:16:36.410
We have to start with
where the students are,

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:37.320
where the children are.

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:40.130
You know, someone once told me
when I was student teaching,

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.500
she was a teacher for
students with disabilities.

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:43.517
She said, "Miguel, don't forget.

00:16:43.517 --> 00:16:46.047
"You teach children, not curriculum.

00:16:46.047 --> 00:16:47.650
"You teach kids, not curriculum."

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:49.520
And that stuck with me.

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:51.070
We need to start with
where the students are.

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:54.820
And right now, they need to
feel a safe school environment.

00:16:54.820 --> 00:16:58.020
They need to have the
relationship, that R,

00:16:58.020 --> 00:16:59.970
before the reading, writing, arithmetic.

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:01.610
I think it can be done together.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:05.900
But we cannot skip past that
social emotional well-being

00:17:05.900 --> 00:17:07.700
of our students, but also our staff.

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:09.940
- That makes a ton of sense.

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:13.230
And then, one extra layer, as you know,

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:15.650
for Khan Academy to do its work,

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:18.610
there needs to be some level
of connectivity devices.

00:17:18.610 --> 00:17:19.970
Obviously, it's not just Khan Academy.

00:17:19.970 --> 00:17:21.330
It opens up a whole new world.

00:17:21.330 --> 00:17:22.910
There's another not-for-profit effort

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:26.080
spawned in reaction to the pandemic

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:27.920
around free tutoring
called schoolhouse.world.

00:17:27.920 --> 00:17:29.190
Once again, kids are not going to be able

00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:34.190
to access things like this
unless they have access.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:35.710
How are you thinking
about the digital divide?

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:37.140
I know the American Rescue Package

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.430
had several billions of dollars for it.

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:42.770
How confident are you that
we're gonna crack that nut?

00:17:42.770 --> 00:17:44.400
- I'm very, I'm very confident.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:47.050
Listen, the digital
divide should be a thing

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:49.190
that's in our rear view mirror soon.

00:17:49.190 --> 00:17:51.930
Yes, it's gonna take bold actions,

00:17:51.930 --> 00:17:54.810
but the bold funding is there
and the direction is there,

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:56.940
and the guidance is that we
have a president that gets it.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:58.090
So let's go.

00:17:58.090 --> 00:17:59.410
Let's close the digital divide.

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.750
We all know the laptop is the new pencil.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:05.175
So let's move forward and let's
open the doors for students.

00:18:05.175 --> 00:18:08.160
That's a basic level of equity
that we need to provide.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:11.750
I know states and communities
are thinking about it.

00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:14.200
That thinking has been
accelerated with the pandemic.

00:18:14.200 --> 00:18:15.910
The tools are there;
the resources are there.

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:17.070
Let's just get it done.

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:19.070
And let's talk about quality instruction

00:18:19.070 --> 00:18:20.390
and recognizing that

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:24.390
that's a tool to get to good
educational opportunity.

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:26.190
So I really feel strongly.

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:27.490
In Connecticut, one of the things we did

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:30.030
is we prioritized with
the governor's leadership

00:18:30.030 --> 00:18:32.550
and the State Department
of Education's leadership.

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:35.220
We prioritized closing that digital divide

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:38.190
because we know that it's not only about

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:41.720
getting the computer up
and the internet access.

00:18:41.720 --> 00:18:44.140
It's also about getting quality content

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:45.530
on the other side of that screen.

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:46.790
And that's critically important.

00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:48.400
That's how gaps are exacerbated,

00:18:48.400 --> 00:18:50.290
when we don't have quality content.

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:53.300
So the first step is getting
a device, good connectivity,

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:54.300
and then quality content.

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.340
Those three things are important.

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:57.540
- It makes a ton of sense.

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:59.590
And I wanna get to everyone's questions,

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:00.790
but I have so many myself.

00:19:00.790 --> 00:19:01.970
So I'm going to indulge myself.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:03.080
You know, y'all,

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:05.320
I've actually never
seen any administration,

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:06.960
and I think the pandemic is part of it,

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:10.940
but I've never seen anyone
with as bold tangible plans

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:12.840
around education as we're starting to see.

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.140
On the American Families
Plan, and I'm reading this,

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:17.897
you know, $200 billion for
free universal preschool,

00:19:17.897 --> 00:19:21.007
$109 billion for free community college,

00:19:21.007 --> 00:19:22.560
$80 billion for Pell grants.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.140
And that's not the complete list.

00:19:24.140 --> 00:19:25.820
But that's a lot of dollars.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.820
You know, $200 billion for
free universal preschool

00:19:28.820 --> 00:19:29.720
for three and four-year olds.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.710
I think there's about 10 or 11 million

00:19:32.710 --> 00:19:33.840
three and four-year olds in the country.

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:36.280
So that comes out to, if
I'm doing the math right,

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:41.280
something like $20,000 per
student to give them that pre-K.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:43.750
And as a father of young children,

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:45.260
I appreciate more than anyone

00:19:45.260 --> 00:19:48.450
how much that creates an
advantage or support for kids.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:51.110
We've invested in it at Khan Academy Kids.

00:19:51.110 --> 00:19:54.650
But how hopeful are you that
these things are going to pass?

00:19:54.650 --> 00:19:56.700
And then how do you see
them getting implemented?

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:58.570
Because there are obviously
a lot of preschools,

00:19:58.570 --> 00:20:01.310
but we don't have a public
preschool infrastructure.

00:20:01.310 --> 00:20:03.260
How do you see that money flowing down?

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:06.690
- So, you know, first and foremost,

00:20:06.690 --> 00:20:10.390
those are technical
strategies that are being

00:20:11.570 --> 00:20:15.380
recommended by the president
who gets what we need to do,

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:17.850
who gets that education is the
foundation of this country,

00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:20.040
that education is the best economic driver

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.630
not only for our country,
but for these individuals.

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:25.040
We know that students are
more likely to graduate

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:27.860
if they have pre-K experiences,

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:30.630
more likely to take higher
level courses, honors courses.

00:20:30.630 --> 00:20:32.730
We know that college graduates,

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:33.860
community college graduates

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:36.690
boost their earning potential by 21%.

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:37.760
Everyone wins here.

00:20:37.760 --> 00:20:39.510
So we have a president that gets it.

00:20:39.510 --> 00:20:41.830
The funds are aimed at providing

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:45.770
an additional four years
of school for the students

00:20:45.770 --> 00:20:47.210
to give them a leg up.

00:20:47.210 --> 00:20:48.960
As he said last night, you know,

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:51.020
we are competing with the world.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:53.490
And he gets that we have to be bold,

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.110
to invest in our students.

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:57.670
I mean, where better can we invest, right,

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.900
in our children across
the country to make sure

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:01.750
that they have the
opportunity to succeed in life

00:21:01.750 --> 00:21:03.630
and that our country can continue to grow.

00:21:03.630 --> 00:21:07.440
I am confident that there's
gonna be support behind this.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:08.920
And I do believe that you're right,

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:10.400
the implementation matters.

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:12.830
But educators have shown this past year

00:21:12.830 --> 00:21:14.380
that they could rise to the challenge

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:16.920
and that they're prepared
to put the students first

00:21:16.920 --> 00:21:18.190
when making decisions.

00:21:18.190 --> 00:21:20.430
And we need to act with
the same level of urgency

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:23.118
that our president has
toward supporting children.

00:21:23.118 --> 00:21:24.656
And we're gonna get it done.

00:21:24.656 --> 00:21:26.200
We're going to get it done.

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:27.210
- Yeah, no, absolutely.

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:28.250
It's very inspiring.

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:29.660
I have a bunch of questions coming in.

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:31.760
These are actually from
two colleagues of mine.

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:33.607
One is Nick Breen on Facebook says,

00:21:33.607 --> 00:21:35.437
"Will the two years of
free community college

00:21:35.437 --> 00:21:37.910
"aspect of the plan be available
to all fields of study?"

00:21:37.910 --> 00:21:39.400
And Kristen DiCerbo,

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:41.060
who is actually Khan Academy's
Chief Learning Officer,

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:42.867
is asking, "We continue to see students

00:21:42.867 --> 00:21:45.087
"entering community colleges
without the reading, writing,

00:21:45.087 --> 00:21:47.017
"and math skills they
need to be successful.

00:21:47.017 --> 00:21:48.167
"Often, these students get stuck

00:21:48.167 --> 00:21:49.357
"in developmental math programs.

00:21:49.357 --> 00:21:50.460
"How do we support these?"

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:53.310
So I kind of view those as
two sides of the same coin.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:54.180
- Fantastic questions.

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:55.940
And I think, you know,
they do connect, right?

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:59.510
So we know that our students
that when they go to college

00:21:59.510 --> 00:22:02.250
and they're still not
reading at grade level,

00:22:02.250 --> 00:22:04.050
there's work that has to
be done at the K-12 level.

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:07.350
So I'm really hopeful that the Rescue Plan

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:09.510
and the CARES Plan really

00:22:09.510 --> 00:22:12.030
and then the budget that the
president is putting forward

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:16.230
supports strong K-12, pre-K-12 learning

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:18.390
and high standards for all
students across the country

00:22:18.390 --> 00:22:19.810
so we don't have that issue.

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:21.740
And then, the community college connection

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:22.650
to the high schools,

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:24.750
we have to make sure that those programs,

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:27.020
while the details of what programs

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:28.460
would be accepted under that,

00:22:28.460 --> 00:22:31.190
what I wanna see, Sal,
is a better connection

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:32.340
between our high school,

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:34.200
actually our middle school, high school,

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:36.510
and two year colleges so
that we provide students

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:38.790
with viable pathways into the workforce

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:41.040
in careers that exist now,

00:22:41.040 --> 00:22:42.980
careers that will exist
five years from now

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:45.830
so that these students can take
advantage of those two years

00:22:45.830 --> 00:22:47.200
and really go into the workforce

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:48.960
or continue to a four-year program

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:50.680
and continue to get their education.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:52.920
Those connections between
the community college

00:22:52.920 --> 00:22:55.880
and our middle and high schools
is one of the goals for me.

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:57.740
There has to be a more clear throughlines

00:22:57.740 --> 00:22:59.420
so more students take advantage of it

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:02.310
and they lead to careers
that these students wanna do

00:23:02.310 --> 00:23:03.483
that are needed now.

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:05.910
- I couldn't agree more.

00:23:05.910 --> 00:23:07.930
You know, one of the
questions that I always wonder

00:23:07.930 --> 00:23:10.190
and this goes off of
Kristen's question is,

00:23:10.190 --> 00:23:11.340
you know the stats.

00:23:11.340 --> 00:23:13.300
Right now, 65%, 70% of all kids

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:14.710
who show up at community college,

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:16.530
they don't even place
into college algebra.

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:17.880
They place into remedial math

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:19.530
which is essentially pre-algebra

00:23:19.530 --> 00:23:21.850
at sixth or seventh grade mathematics.

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:24.580
And we also know that college algebra,

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:25.720
one, when you don't place into it,

00:23:25.720 --> 00:23:27.620
it's a huge signal that
you're not gonna graduate

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:28.570
and you're gonna have debt,

00:23:28.570 --> 00:23:30.430
and that college algebra itself

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:33.690
is a huge gating factor for
a lot of kids, even though

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:35.960
it really should have been
taught in ninth or 10th grade.

00:23:35.960 --> 00:23:37.640
Algebra one and algebra two

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:39.783
are essentially college algebra.

00:23:41.463 --> 00:23:42.590
And I love your advice,

00:23:42.590 --> 00:23:45.040
that Khan Academy and this
free tutoring platform,

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:46.280
schoolhouse.world we're creating,

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:49.290
how it can help teachers and students get

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.230
maybe ways we can
partner with universities

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:52.550
to get college algebra credit

00:23:52.550 --> 00:23:55.630
before they even leave high school.

00:23:55.630 --> 00:23:56.920
Are you thinking things like that

00:23:56.920 --> 00:23:59.210
so that college algebra
should just be off the table,

00:23:59.210 --> 00:24:01.970
so it shouldn't be a
gating factor anymore?

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:03.760
- You know, one of the things
we learned in this pandemic

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:05.750
is we have to think outside the box.

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:07.850
So we need to really make sure that we're

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:10.190
providing opportunities for
students to learn these things.

00:24:10.190 --> 00:24:12.370
And it doesn't have to be
during the regular school day.

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:14.530
It could be, we could be creative.

00:24:14.530 --> 00:24:16.990
But I wanna take it back
a little bit further.

00:24:16.990 --> 00:24:19.380
They're not accessing those classes,

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:22.350
they're not passing those
classes in eighth or ninth grade

00:24:22.350 --> 00:24:24.510
because maybe in third grade,

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:26.580
they were at a first-grade reading level.

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:27.860
And maybe in first grade,

00:24:27.860 --> 00:24:29.310
they were still learning their letters.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:34.310
So this universal pre-kindergarten
and early childhood focus

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:36.990
that the president is providing here

00:24:36.990 --> 00:24:39.680
will help alleviate some
of those issues as well.

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:41.370
So yes, we have to get
creative for those students

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:44.690
that are underserved and
that we haven't gotten

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:46.540
to that point where they're
passing those courses.

00:24:46.540 --> 00:24:48.140
But when we have a strong foundation

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.490
in early childhood
education, it's less likely

00:24:50.490 --> 00:24:52.280
that that pattern is gonna continue.

00:24:52.280 --> 00:24:55.500
So I'm really pleased with that
focus from President Biden.

00:24:55.500 --> 00:24:57.660
And I know that if implemented well,

00:24:57.660 --> 00:24:59.600
those issues are gonna start going away

00:24:59.600 --> 00:25:01.610
because we're gonna better
prepare our students early.

00:25:01.610 --> 00:25:02.900
And the gaps that we see

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:04.840
when our three and four
and five-year-olds come in

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:06.920
are gonna be diminished
when we have strong programs

00:25:06.920 --> 00:25:08.340
at the early childhood level.

00:25:08.340 --> 00:25:09.290
It's all connected.

00:25:10.670 --> 00:25:13.480
- And I think I could
predict what you might say,

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:16.220
but what's your view on
the student debt crisis?

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:17.150
I didn't see that.

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:19.330
I mean, it's implicitly
addressed in some of the things

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:21.670
like free community college
and more Pell grants.

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:23.750
But we know it's over a
trillion dollars of debt.

00:25:23.750 --> 00:25:26.910
I have even people in my
family who are in their 40s

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:28.380
who are still paying off debt

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:30.230
or maybe even in some cases given up.

00:25:33.023 --> 00:25:37.020
What can we do there that can
help unlock a lot of folks?

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:37.950
- That's a major issue.

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:39.900
And you know, it's a two-pronged approach.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.370
The first prong is making
sure we can do what we can

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:44.590
to support students that are in debt now.

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:46.940
We're having conversations
about what that might look like.

00:25:46.940 --> 00:25:48.060
But the second step to that

00:25:48.060 --> 00:25:49.800
I don't think gets discussed as much

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:51.500
but we really need to focus on

00:25:51.500 --> 00:25:53.550
is ensuring that this
doesn't happen again,

00:25:53.550 --> 00:25:55.630
that we have public
service loan forgiveness

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:56.970
that actually works.

00:25:56.970 --> 00:25:59.110
You know, we've had a system that rejects

00:25:59.110 --> 00:26:01.510
like 98% of the applicants.

00:26:01.510 --> 00:26:02.890
That's unacceptable.

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:04.600
We have to do borrower defense better.

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:06.130
We have to make sure that our colleges

00:26:06.130 --> 00:26:08.500
are providing a good return on investment

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:10.140
for these learners that are coming in,

00:26:10.140 --> 00:26:11.750
especially these first gen students.

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:14.830
You know, I read a fact recently that

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:19.030
some borrowers are in
greater debt 12 years later

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:21.340
because they were never able
to knock down the principal.

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:23.310
So we have to fix that.

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:25.060
We have to make sure we
have pathways to college

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:28.490
that are affordable so more
students take advantage of that.

00:26:28.490 --> 00:26:29.450
So it's a two-pronged approach.

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:31.210
Yes, we're gonna look at

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:33.120
potential loan forgiveness conversation

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:35.000
and making sure that
we're supporting those

00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:36.440
who are in major debt.

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:37.380
But we're also gonna make sure

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:40.730
that we're stopping the bleeding
by promoting good practices

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:44.320
that alleviate some of
this debt accumulation

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:45.720
that we see in our students.

00:26:46.710 --> 00:26:48.750
- Yeah, and it seems like
even our bankruptcy laws

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:49.583
are a little bit strange.

00:26:49.583 --> 00:26:50.780
It's easier to cancel your debt

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:52.620
when you max out a credit card

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:54.430
or you run a business into the ground

00:26:54.430 --> 00:26:57.900
than, you know, if
you're not able to cover

00:26:57.900 --> 00:26:58.860
all of your student debt.

00:26:58.860 --> 00:26:59.693
There should be a penalty,

00:26:59.693 --> 00:27:01.240
which is bankruptcy or
your credit score gets,

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:02.370
but at least there's a way.

00:27:02.370 --> 00:27:04.860
That's why bankruptcy exists, to reboot.

00:27:04.860 --> 00:27:06.430
- I'm excited about bringing folks onboard

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:07.840
that have a student-centered approach

00:27:07.840 --> 00:27:09.470
to the policies that we're making

00:27:09.470 --> 00:27:11.180
and ensuring that we're
not in this position again

00:27:11.180 --> 00:27:12.013
in five years.

00:27:12.013 --> 00:27:13.930
Absolutely, I'm looking
forward to having more

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:15.130
work done on this issue.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:19.142
- No, I mean, it's very
inspiring to hear that.

00:27:19.142 --> 00:27:20.150
You know, I know we're almost out of time.

00:27:20.150 --> 00:27:21.910
You're super busy.

00:27:21.910 --> 00:27:23.470
First of all, let me just say

00:27:23.470 --> 00:27:26.280
I consider what we're
doing at Khan Academy,

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:28.200
what we're trying to do
at schoolhouse.world,

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:29.620
these are in service

00:27:29.620 --> 00:27:30.990
to hopefully the same mission and vision.

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:33.770
So, you know, consider us at your service

00:27:33.770 --> 00:27:36.920
of how we can move the dial
for learners and teachers

00:27:36.920 --> 00:27:38.180
and parents in America.

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:39.510
But I'd love to, you know,

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:41.260
for you to finish up with
any messages you have.

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:43.120
We have a lot of teachers,
a lot of parents,

00:27:43.120 --> 00:27:45.500
a lot of children watching right now.

00:27:45.500 --> 00:27:46.333
- Thank you very much.

00:27:46.333 --> 00:27:47.166
And thanks for the invite.

00:27:47.166 --> 00:27:49.020
It was nice to be, this
was a long Homeroom.

00:27:49.020 --> 00:27:50.730
Usually, Homeroom is about 10 minutes.

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:52.560
So this is nice that we
had a 30-minute Homeroom.

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:55.260
But my message is, first
of all, thank you, Sal,

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:57.000
for everything that
you've done in education

00:27:57.000 --> 00:27:58.510
in supporting educators,

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.810
supporting students across the country.

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:01.643
Really appreciate it.

00:28:01.643 --> 00:28:04.030
My own children, I have a
14-year old and a 16-year old

00:28:04.030 --> 00:28:06.420
who benefit from Khan Academy.

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:08.280
And I just wanna say thank
you for what you've done.

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:10.930
But to the educators and the families,

00:28:10.930 --> 00:28:13.090
I wanna say thank you for what
you've done this past year.

00:28:13.090 --> 00:28:15.320
But know we have an opportunity
to hit the reset button

00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:17.110
and really elevate our practices

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:19.600
in making sure that we're
supporting our educators,

00:28:19.600 --> 00:28:21.360
supporting our students in different ways

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:25.560
so that all students have
access to a quality education.

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:26.470
And we're gonna do it together.

00:28:26.470 --> 00:28:28.820
One thing that I learned,
that intentional collaboration

00:28:28.820 --> 00:28:31.210
is how we're gonna move
ahead as a country.

00:28:31.210 --> 00:28:33.750
So I look forward to
working with you, Sal,

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:35.640
with the families, with
educators to make sure

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:37.580
we can do the very best
for all of the students

00:28:37.580 --> 00:28:38.413
across the country.

00:28:38.413 --> 00:28:40.040
Again, thanks for letting me be on.

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:43.090
- Well, Secretary Cardona,
this was a real honor.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:44.830
And I'll say it again, your energy,

00:28:44.830 --> 00:28:47.030
you know, you're you're only 59 days in

00:28:47.030 --> 00:28:49.000
but your energy and your
background and your experience

00:28:49.000 --> 00:28:51.940
gives me very high hopes
for what we're as a country

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:53.250
going to be able to accomplish together.

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:55.640
So thank you so much
for your service here.

00:28:55.640 --> 00:28:56.790
- All right, take care.

